LLM-Assisted First-Principles Rocket Engine Design

A Falsifiable Computational Framework for Physics-Constrained Architecture Exploration

Version 0.4 — Formal Grammar, Statistical Rigor, and Verification Architecture

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: Claude, Deepseek, Meta, chatGpt 

Abstract

This document specifies Version 0.4 of a computational framework for exploring liquid rocket engine architectures under physics-based verification budgets. The central falsifiable question remains: does LLM-generated architectural prior reduce the number of physics-solver evaluations required to reach a Pareto-optimal design, compared to random and heuristic initialization?

V0.4 addresses six critical issues identified in V0.3: (i) the hypergraph grammar formalism is made rigorous with port-typed hyperedges and attachment nodes, eliminating the generation of chemically nonsensical graphs; (ii) the statistical power analysis is corrected, with revised sample-size requirements; (iii) the hypervolume reference point is fixed a priori, removing normalization leakage; (iv) the objective vector is reduced to an independent set, eliminating the T/W redundancy; (v) NSGA-II constraint handling is specified with ε-constraint relaxation and repair operators applied equally to all arms; and (vi) the entropy generation validity gate is separated from the entropy minimization objective. Rotordynamic and structural constraints are added, and the Groeneveld CHF table is reclassified as an extrapolation with explicit uncertainty.

Every mathematical claim is stated with proof sketch or citation. Every physical constraint is enforced by a solver. The LLM remains a stochastic proposal generator whose outputs are hypotheses to be falsified by independent numerical solvers.

