2. Formal Design-Space Definition

2.1 Hypergraph Grammar with Port Typing

The design space is defined by a port-typed hyperedge replacement grammar (HRG) $\mathcal{G} = (N, \Sigma, R, S)$, where:

· $N$ is a finite set of nonterminal hyperedge labels (each with a fixed arity $k = \text{type}(e)$);
· $\Sigma$ is a finite set of terminal hyperedge labels;
· $R$ is a finite set of productions $p: A \to H$, where $A \in N$ is a nonterminal hyperedge of arity $k$, and $H$ is a hypergraph with $|ext_H| = k$ external nodes;
· $S \in N$ is the start symbol.

A hypergraph is a tuple $H = (V_H, E_H, att_H, lab_H, ext_H)$ where $V_H$ is a finite set of nodes, $E_H$ is a finite set of hyperedges, $att_H: E_H \to V_H^*$ is the attachment function, $lab_H: E_H \to \Sigma$ is the labelling function, and $ext_H \in V_H^*$ are the external nodes.

Port typing. To prevent chemically nonsensical graphs (e.g., oxidizer flow into a fuel pump), every attachment node carries a port type $\pi \in \Pi$:

\Pi = \{\text{FuelIn}, \text{FuelOut}, \text{OxIn}, \text{OxOut}, \text{HotGasIn}, \text{HotGasOut}, \text{ShaftMechanical}, \text{CoolantIn}, \text{CoolantOut}, \text{Structural}\}

A production $A \to H$ is well-typed if for every hyperedge $e \in E_H$ and every attachment position $i$, the port type of $att_H(e)[i]$ in $H$ matches the port type required by the corresponding external node of $H$. The grammar is port-consistent if all productions are well-typed. This is enforced by construction.

Component types (nodes with ports):

Component Input Ports Output Ports
Tank — FuelOut or OxOut
Pump FuelIn or OxIn FuelOut or OxOut (at higher pressure)
Turbine HotGasIn HotGasOut, ShaftMechanical
Preburner FuelIn, OxIn HotGasOut
Chamber FuelIn, OxIn (via Injector) HotGasOut
Nozzle HotGasIn —
Valve FluidIn FluidOut (same fluid)
FeedLine FluidIn FluidOut (same fluid)
Junction FluidIn × k FluidOut × m (mass conservation)

Example production rules:

Rule LHS RHS Description
$p_1$ Engine (arity 0) Tank(FuelOut) → Pump(FuelIn, FuelOut) → Chamber(FuelIn, OxIn, HotGasOut) → Nozzle(HotGasIn) Gas generator backbone
$p_2$ Pump (arity 2) PumpBody with ShaftMechanical port Turbopump assembly
$p_3$ Chamber (arity 3) Injector(FuelIn, OxIn) → ChamberBody → Nozzle Thrust chamber assembly
$p_4$ Engine (arity 0) Preburner(FuelIn, OxIn, HotGasOut) → Turbine(HotGasIn, ShaftMechanical) Staged combustion addition
$p_5$ FeedLine (arity 2) FeedLine → Valve(FluidIn, FluidOut) → FeedLine Valve insertion (recursive, bounded by $L_{\max}$)

Grammar-generated design space:

\mathcal{A} = \{G : S \Rightarrow^* G\}

where $\Rightarrow^*$ is the reflexive-transitive closure of the rewriting relation. Each architecture $A \in \mathcal{A}$ is a port-typed labeled directed hypergraph.

Isomorphic deduplication. Different derivation sequences can yield isomorphic hypergraphs. For population diversity and cardinality interpretation, we canonicalize each generated architecture to a canonical form under hypergraph isomorphism. Two hypergraphs $G, H$ are isomorphic if there exists a bijective external-node-reflecting hypergraph morphism $G \to H$. Canonical labeling is performed via a color-refinement algorithm followed by individualization-refinement, yielding a unique representative per isomorphism class. The effective design space is:

\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}} = \mathcal{A} / \cong

2.2 Measure-Theoretic Foundation

Let $\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}$ be the finite set of isomorphism classes of grammatically valid architectures, and let $\mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ be the parameter space. The design space is:

\Omega = \mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}} \times \mathcal{X}

equipped with the product $\sigma$-algebra $\Sigma = \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}) \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X})$.

Objective function: We define an independent objective vector $\mathbf{f}: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^m$ as:

\mathbf{f}(A, \mathbf{x}) = \left[-F, -I_{sp}, m_{\text{engine}}, C_{\text{complexity}}, \dot{S}_{\text{gen}}^{\text{opt}}\right]

where:

· $F$ is vacuum thrust (maximize);
· $I_{sp}$ is vacuum specific impulse (maximize);
· $m_{\text{engine}}$ is engine dry mass (minimize);
· $C_{\text{complexity}}(A)$ is structural complexity (minimize);
· $\dot{S}_{\text{gen}}^{\text{opt}}$ is the optimization entropy objective, defined in Section 3.4, which excludes components where the solver validity gate fails.

Note: $T/W = F/(m_{\text{engine}} g)$ is not a separate objective. It is derived from $F$ and $m_{\text{engine}}$ and is reported as a diagnostic only.

Constraint functions: The feasible set is:

\Omega_{\text{feas}} = \{(A, \mathbf{x}) \in \Omega : g_i(A, \mathbf{x}) \leq 0, \; i = 1, \ldots, n_c; \; |h_j(A, \mathbf{x})| \leq \epsilon_{\text{eq}}, \; j = 1, \ldots, n_e\}

where equality constraints are relaxed to $|h_j| \leq \epsilon_{\text{eq}}$ with adaptive $\epsilon_{\text{eq}}$ (Section 5.4).

2.3 Complexity of the Design Space

Proposition 1 (Cardinality bound). If the grammar $\mathcal{G}$ has $p$ production rules and the maximum derivation depth is $L_{\max}$, then:

|\mathcal{A}| \leq p^{L_{\max}}

Proof. Each derivation is a sequence of at most $L_{\max}$ rule applications with at most $p$ choices each. Different sequences may yield the same hypergraph, so this is an upper bound. $\square$

For $p = 20$ and $L_{\max} = 8$: $|\mathcal{A}| \leq 2.56 \times 10^{10}$.

Effective cardinality. $|\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}| \ll |\mathcal{A}|$ due to isomorphism collapse and port-type constraints. The exact ratio is measured empirically for the specific grammar. This ratio is reported as the grammar compression factor $\gamma_{\text{gram}} = |\mathcal{A}| / |\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}|$.

Full-flow staged combustion feasibility. Before committing to V0.6, we verify that the grammar generates full-flow staged combustion with dual preburners. This requires:

· Two independent preburner derivations (one fuel-rich, one oxidizer-rich);
· Two independent turbine derivations;
· Both turbine shafts mechanically coupled to the corresponding pumps;
· Both preburner exhausts routed to the main chamber.

This topology is a strict superset of the gas-generator and single-preburner staged combustion topologies. If the grammar cannot generate it with $L_{\max} \leq 12$, $L_{\max}$ is increased.

