3. Governing Equations and Thermodynamic Foundations

3.1 Conservation Laws per Control Volume

For every control volume $C$ (feed line, pump, turbine, preburner, chamber, nozzle):

Mass conservation (steady):
\sum_{i \in \text{in}(C)} \dot{m}_i = \sum_{j \in \text{out}(C)} \dot{m}_j

First law (steady, with shaft work):
\dot{Q} - \dot{W} + \dot{m}\left(h_{\text{in}} + \frac{V_{\text{in}}^2}{2} + g z_{\text{in}}\right) = \dot{m}\left(h_{\text{out}} + \frac{V_{\text{out}}^2}{2} + g z_{\text{out}}\right)

Second law (entropy generation):
\dot{S}_{\text{gen}} = \sum_j \dot{m}_j s_j - \sum_i \dot{m}_i s_i - \sum_k \frac{\dot{Q}_k}{T_k}

Momentum conservation (1D, steady, with friction):
\frac{dP}{dx} = -\rho V \frac{dV}{dx} - \frac{f}{D_h}\frac{\rho V^2}{2}

3.2 Validity Gate vs. Optimization Objective

Critical distinction. V0.3 conflated two distinct uses of $\dot{S}_{\text{gen}}$. V0.4 separates them:

Validity gate (solver self-consistency): For every control volume $C$, the component model must satisfy:
\dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C} \geq -\varepsilon_{\text{num}}


where $\varepsilon_{\text{num}} = 10^{-6}$ (relative to total entropy flow) is the numerical tolerance. If $\dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C} < -\varepsilon_{\text{num}}$, the component model has produced a physically invalid result (a solver bug). The design is rejected from the optimization population and logged as a solver failure. This is a self-consistency check on the numerical model, not a thermodynamic quality metric.

Optimization objective (thermodynamic cost): For designs that pass the validity gate, the total entropy generation is:
\dot{S}_{\text{gen}}^{\text{opt}} = \sum_{C: \text{valid}} \dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C}


This is minimized as an objective. Because the validity gate ensures $\dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C} \geq 0$ for all included components, the optimizer cannot "learn to avoid" regions where the solver breaks — those regions are excluded before the objective is computed.

Justification for optimization: Entropy generation minimization as a thermodynamic cost metric for rocket cycles is established in the literature. However, it is a relative metric: lower $\dot{S}_{\text{gen}}$ indicates a cycle closer to the reversible limit, but does not guarantee higher $I_{sp}$ or thrust. It is included as an independent axis for Pareto exploration, not as a proxy for performance.

3.3 Real-Fluid Equation of State

For cryogenic methane and oxygen at supercritical conditions, the Peng–Robinson EOS is employed:

P = \frac{RT}{v - b} - \frac{a(T)}{v^2 + 2bv - b^2}

with $a(T) = 0.45724 \frac{R^2 T_c^2}{P_c} \alpha(T)$, $b = 0.07780 \frac{R T_c}{P_c}$, and $\alpha(T) = [1 + \kappa(1 - \sqrt{T/T_c})]^2$.

Enthalpy and entropy departures are computed as:

h - h^{\text{ig}} = RT(Z - 1) + \frac{T \frac{da}{dT} - a}{2\sqrt{2}b}\ln\left[\frac{Z + (1+\sqrt{2})B}{Z + (1-\sqrt{2})B}\right]

s - s^{\text{ig}} = R\ln(Z - B) + \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}b}\frac{da}{dT}\ln\left[\frac{Z + (1+\sqrt{2})B}{Z + (1-\sqrt{2})B}\right]

3.4 Turbomachinery Thermodynamics

Pump work (real-fluid enthalpy-based):
\dot{W}_p = \frac{\dot{m}(h_{\text{out},s} - h_{\text{in}})}{\eta_p}

Specific speed:
N_s = \frac{N \sqrt{Q}}{H^{3/4}}

where $N$ is in rpm, $Q$ in m³/s, and $H$ in m. PUMPA meanline correlations provide validated pump maps for off-design analysis.

Turbine work:
\dot{W}_t = \dot{m}_t \eta_t \Delta h_t^{\text{ideal}}

3.5 Combustion Model

Gibbs free-energy minimization:

\min_{\{n_i\}} G = \sum_i n_i \mu_i(T, P) \quad \text{subject to} \quad \sum_i a_{ij} n_i = b_j \quad \forall j

Cantera vs. CEA validation. Cantera 0D equilibrium with ideal-gas assumptions has been shown to compare favorably with NASA CEA for LOx/CH₄ equilibrium at moderate pressures, with disparities increasing at the highest equivalence ratios. At $P_c > 100$ bar, Cantera with ideal-gas EOS may deviate. V0.4 uses NASA CEA via rocketcea as the primary equilibrium solver and Cantera with high-pressure transport and real-gas EOS as the verification model, with tolerance ±2% in $c^*$ and ±75 K in $T_c$ for $P_c > 100$ bar, and ±1% / ±30 K below that.

3.6 Injector–Chamber Coupling

\dot{m}_{\text{inj}} = C_d A_{\text{inj}} \sqrt{2 \rho \Delta P_{\text{inj}}}

Resolution of V0.3 contradiction: $C_d$ is a correlation $C_d(Re, L/D)$ from Sutton/Biblarz with an epistemic uncertainty band of $\pm 10\%$ around the correlation prediction. The uncertainty is propagated via Monte Carlo, not treated as a free optimization parameter.

Chugging stability:
\frac{\Delta P_{\text{inj}}}{P_c} \geq \kappa_{\text{chug}}, \qquad \kappa_{\text{chug}} = 0.20 \pm 0.05

3.7 Acoustic Stability Screen

Chamber acoustic modes:
f_{mnq} = \frac{c_c}{2}\sqrt{\left(\frac{\alpha_{mn}}{\pi R_c}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{q}{L_c}\right)^2}

with boundary conditions closed–open (injector end closed, nozzle end open). Rayleigh criterion screening:

\alpha_{\text{growth}} \propto \int_V p' \dot{q}' \, dV

Designs with $\alpha_{\text{growth}} > \alpha_{\text{damping}}$ are rejected. This is a screening tool only; passing designs are labeled "stability-compatible under low-order screening."

