4. Structural and Rotordynamic Constraints

4.1 Structural Constraints (Added in V0.4)

V0.3 omitted structural constraints beyond wall temperature. V0.4 adds:

Constraint Formulation Source
Hoop stress $\sigma_\theta = P_c R / t \leq \sigma_{\text{yield}} / FS$ Thin-wall theory
Buckling $P_{\text{external}} \leq P_{\text{cr}} = \frac{2E}{\sqrt{3(1-\nu^2)}} (t/R)^2$ Classical shell buckling
Fatigue life $N_{\text{cycles}} \geq N_{\text{mission}}$ Coffin–Manson (simplified)
Flange load $F_{\text{flange}} \leq F_{\text{allow}}$ Bolt preload

where $FS = 1.5$ is the factor of safety, $\sigma_{\text{yield}}$ is temperature-dependent yield strength with $\sigma = 5\%$ uncertainty, and $E$ is Young's modulus.

4.2 Rotordynamic Constraints (Added in V0.4)

For each turbopump shaft, the first bending critical speed must exceed the operating speed by a sufficient margin. Rotordynamic analysis of liquid rocket turbopumps shows that the first critical speed is typically 5–10% of nominal shaft speed for rigid-body modes, with bending modes at higher speeds. The rear bearing stiffness is the most important parameter for critical speed and instability.

Critical speed constraint:
|N_{\text{operating}} - N_{\text{critical},i}| \geq \Delta N_{\text{margin}}, \qquad \Delta N_{\text{margin}} = 0.20 \, N_{\text{operating}}

Bearing load constraint:
F_{\text{bearing}} \leq F_{\text{bearing,allow}}

Rotor stability: The logarithmic decrement $\delta$ must satisfy $\delta > 0$ at the operating speed, computed from the complex eigenvalue analysis of the rotor–bearing–seal system.

Simplified model for V0.4: A one-dimensional transfer matrix method with lumped masses, bearing stiffness/damping, and seal cross-coupling coefficients. This is a screening model; detailed rotordynamic analysis is deferred to V0.5.

