5. Optimization Architecture

5.1 NSGA-II with Graph-Grammar Encoding

The optimization algorithm is NSGA-II. Each individual is a pair $(G, \mathbf{x})$ where $G$ is a port-typed hypergraph generated by the grammar and $\mathbf{x}$ is the continuous parameter vector.

Operator Graph Component Parameter Component
Crossover Rule-sequence recombination Simulated binary crossover (SBX)
Mutation Rule insertion/deletion (port-consistent only) Polynomial mutation
Repair Grammar-constrained repair Bound clamping + constraint repair

5.2 LLM Integration Architecture

The LLM acts as a stochastic proposal generator producing JSON conforming to a schema. Integration points:

Stage LLM Role Frequency
Initialization Propose initial population Once per run
Exploration Propose novel architectures Every $k$ generations
Exploitation Mutate promising architectures Every $k$ generations
Repair Suggest parameter adjustments On constraint violation

JSON parsing reliability. Published workflows report ~30% of LLM engineering proposals fail JSON validation. V0.4 uses constrained decoding with a JSON schema grammar, which enforces structural validity during generation rather than post-hoc validation. Residual failures are repaired via a schema-guided repair loop. The number of LLM calls, tokens consumed, and failed proposals are logged.

5.3 Constraint Handling in NSGA-II

Equality constraints. NSGA-II does not natively handle equality constraints. V0.4 uses ε-constraint relaxation with adaptive ε:

|h_j(A, \mathbf{x})| \leq \epsilon_{\text{eq}}(t)

where $\epsilon_{\text{eq}}(t)$ decreases from $10^{-1}$ to $10^{-6}$ over generations. The turbomachinery matching equations (shaft power balance, mass conservation) are equality constraints.

Repair mechanism confound. V0.3 gave the LLM arm a constraint-repair loop but did not give equivalent repair to baselines. V0.4 gives all arms an identical numerical repair operator: a gradient-based projection onto the feasible set for continuous parameters, and a grammar-guided repair for graph components. The repair operator is:

1. Compute constraint violation $\phi(\mathbf{z}) = \sum_j \max(0, g_j(\mathbf{z})) + \sum_k |h_k(\mathbf{z})|$;
2. If $\phi > 0$, compute the gradient $\nabla \phi$ (finite differences for graph components);
3. Project $\mathbf{z}$ onto the feasible set via constrained gradient descent (5 iterations).

This ensures that any performance difference between LLM and random arms is attributable to proposal quality, not to asymmetric access to repair.

5.4 Structural Complexity Metric

C_{\text{complexity}}(A) = w_n |V| + w_h |H| + w_s n_{\text{shafts}} + w_p n_{\text{preburners}}

with $w_n = w_h = 1$, $w_s = 3$, $w_p = 5$ as baseline values. Sensitivity analysis is reported for $\pm 50\%$ variation in each weight.

5.5 Shaft-Level Matching

For each shaft $k$:
\dot{W}_{t,k}(\dot{m}_{t,k}, \Pi_{t,k}) = \dot{W}_{p,k}(\dot{m}_{p,k}, \Delta P_{p,k}) + \dot{W}_{\text{aux},k}

subject to $\dot{m}_{t,k} = \dot{m}_{p,k} + \dot{m}_{\text{bleed},k}$.

Solved by Newton–Raphson with Jacobian conditioning check. If $\text{cond}(J) > 10^6$, the shaft is rejected as ill-conditioned.

Cavitation constraint:
P_{\text{in}} \geq P_{\text{vapor}}(T_{\text{in}}) + \rho g \cdot \text{NPSH}_{\text{required}}(\dot{m}, N)

NPSH is interpolated from the PUMPA-generated pump map.

