13. Mathematical Appendix: Proofs

A.1 Pareto Compliance of Hypervolume

Theorem. If $A \succ B$, then $\mathcal{H}(A, \mathbf{r}) \geq \mathcal{H}(B, \mathbf{r})$ for any $\mathbf{r}$ dominating $A \cup B$.

Proof. For every $\mathbf{b} \in B$, there exists $\mathbf{a} \in A$ with $\mathbf{a} \leq \mathbf{b}$. Hence $[\mathbf{b}, \mathbf{r}] \subseteq [\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{r}]$, and the union over $B$ is contained in the union over $A$. Taking Lebesgue measure gives $\mathcal{H}(B, \mathbf{r}) \leq \mathcal{H}(A, \mathbf{r})$. $\square$

A.2 Newton–Raphson Convergence for Shaft Matching

Theorem. If $\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{z}) = \mathbf{0}$ is continuously differentiable near $\mathbf{z}^*$ and $J(\mathbf{z}^*)$ is nonsingular, Newton–Raphson converges quadratically to $\mathbf{z}^*$.

Proof. Standard Newton–Raphson convergence theorem. The Jacobian nonsingularity is checked numerically; if $\text{cond}(J) > 10^6$, the shaft is rejected as ill-conditioned. $\square$

A.3 Port-Type Safety

Theorem. If all productions of grammar $\mathcal{G}$ are port-consistent, then every derived architecture $A \in \mathcal{A}$ has port-type-consistent connections.

Proof. By induction on derivation length. Base case: the start symbol $S$ has no attachments. Inductive step: assume $G$ is port-consistent. Applying a port-consistent production $A \to H$ replaces a hyperedge $e$ with $H$; the external nodes of $H$ match the attachment nodes of $e$ by construction, so port types are preserved. $\square$

A.4 Isomorphism Collapse Bound

Proposition. For a grammar with $p$ rules and $L_{\max}$ depth, $|\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}| \leq p^{L_{\max}}$, with equality iff no two derivations yield isomorphic hypergraphs.

Proof. The map from derivation sequences to isomorphism classes is surjective, so $|\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}| \leq |\text{derivations}| \leq p^{L_{\max}}$. $\square$

