14. Research Philosophy

Principle 1 — Physics before language. The equations constrain the LLM. The LLM never overrides a solver result.

Principle 2 — Independent verification. The LLM cannot validate its own claims. Verification uses independent physical models.

Principle 3 — Explicit uncertainty. Unknown quantities remain labeled. Their uncertainty is propagated, not ignored. Extrapolated correlations are tagged [ASSUMED-EXTRAPOLATED].

Principle 4 — Reproducibility. Every result is reproducible from inputs, equations, code, and parameters. Every LLM interaction is logged.

Principle 5 — Constrained exploration, not discovery. The framework explores a design space defined by the researcher's formal grammar. It does not discover architectures outside that space.

Principle 6 — Falsifiability. Every claim is stated in a form that admits a test. A claim that cannot be falsified is not a scientific claim.

Principle 7 — Symmetric comparison. All experimental arms receive identical repair operators, constraint handling, and computational resources. Any performance difference is attributable to proposal quality.

Principle 8 — Validity before optimization. A solver's numerical self-consistency is verified before its output is used as an optimization objective.

