Appendix A: Hypergraph Replacement Grammar Formalism

A.1 Formal Definition of Port-Typed HRG

Definition A.1 (Hypergraph). A hypergraph is a tuple  H = (V_H, E_H, att_H, lab_H, ext_H)  where:

·  V_H  is a finite set of nodes;
·  E_H  is a finite set of hyperedges;
·  att_H: E_H \to V_H^*  is the attachment function mapping each hyperedge to a sequence of nodes;
·  lab_H: E_H \to \Sigma  is a labelling function over a finite label alphabet  \Sigma ;
·  ext_H \in V_H^*  is a sequence of pairwise distinct external nodes.

Hyperedge replacement grammars generalize context-free string grammars to graphs: a production rule replaces a hyperedge with a hypergraph, where the external nodes of the right-hand side are identified with the attachment nodes of the replaced hyperedge. This approach is elementary and structurally analogous to context-free string grammars, with well-studied structural properties and decision problems including membership.

Definition A.2 (Port-Typed HRG). A port-typed hyperedge replacement grammar is a tuple  \mathcal{G} = (N, \Sigma, R, S)  where:

·  N  is a finite set of nonterminal hyperedge labels, each with a fixed arity  k = \text{type}(e) ;
·  \Sigma  is a finite set of terminal hyperedge labels;
·  R  is a finite set of productions  p: A \to H , where  A \in N  is a nonterminal hyperedge of arity  k , and  H  is a hypergraph with  |ext_H| = k  external nodes;
·  S \in N  is the start symbol.

Definition A.3 (Port types). Every attachment node carries a port type  \pi \in \Pi :

\Pi = \{\text{FuelIn}, \text{FuelOut}, \text{OxIn}, \text{OxOut}, \text{HotGasIn}, \text{HotGasOut}, \text{ShaftMechanical}, \text{CoolantIn}, \text{CoolantOut}, \text{Structural}\}

A production  A \to H  is well-typed if for every hyperedge  e \in E_H  and every attachment position  i , the port type of  att_H(e)[i]  in  H  matches the port type required by the corresponding external node of  H .

A.2 Well-Typedness Theorems

Theorem A.1 (Port-Type Safety). If all productions of grammar  \mathcal{G}  are port-consistent, then every derived architecture  A \in \mathcal{A}  has port-type-consistent connections.

Proof. By induction on derivation length.

Base case: The start symbol  S  has no attachments; the property holds vacuously.

Inductive step: Assume  G  is port-consistent. Applying a port-consistent production  A \to H  replaces a hyperedge  e  with  H . The external nodes of  H  match the attachment nodes of  e  by construction. Since all productions are port-consistent, the port types at every connection in  H  match the types required by the replaced hyperedge. The resulting graph  G'  is port-consistent.  \square 

Theorem A.2 (Non-Generation of Chemically Nonsensical Graphs). Under the port-typed grammar, the derivation  \text{FuelTank} \to \text{OxPump}  is impossible.

Proof. The hyperedge connecting a fuel tank to a pump requires the pump's inlet port to be of type  \text{FuelIn} . The oxidizer pump has inlet port type  \text{OxIn} . Since  \text{FuelIn} \neq \text{OxIn} , no well-typed production can produce this connection.  \square 

A.3 Isomorphism Deduplication

Definition A.4 (Hypergraph Isomorphism). Two hypergraphs  G = (V_G, E_G, att_G, lab_G, ext_G)  and  H = (V_H, E_H, att_H, lab_H, ext_H)  are isomorphic if there exist bijections  \phi_V: V_G \to V_H  and  \phi_E: E_G \to E_H  such that:

·  lab_G(e) = lab_H(\phi_E(e))  for all  e \in E_G ;
·  att_H(\phi_E(e)) = \phi_V(att_G(e))  for all  e \in E_G ;
·  \phi_V(ext_G) = ext_H .

Canonical labeling via color refinement. The color refinement algorithm (1-dimensional Weisfeiler-Leman) iteratively refines a coloring of vertices by hashing the multiset of colors of neighboring vertices. Individualization-refinement extends this by individualizing a vertex (assigning it a unique color) and repeating refinement until a discrete coloring is obtained. This is the standard method for symmetry computations of graphs and combinatorial objects.

Compression factor. The grammar compression factor is:

\gamma_{\text{gram}} = \frac{|\mathcal{A}|}{|\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}|}

where  \mathcal{A}  is the set of derivation sequences and  \mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}} = \mathcal{A}/\cong  is the set of isomorphism classes.

Proposition A.1 (Isomorphism Collapse Bound). For a grammar with  p  rules and maximum depth  L_{\max} :

|\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}| \leq p^{L_{\max}}

with equality iff no two distinct derivation sequences yield isomorphic hypergraphs.

Proof. The map from derivation sequences to isomorphism classes is surjective by definition. Hence  |\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}| \leq |\text{derivations}| \leq p^{L_{\max}} .  \square 

A.4 Grammar Complexity and Full-Flow Staged Combustion

Proposition A.2 (Cardinality Bound). If the grammar  \mathcal{G}  has  p  production rules and the maximum derivation depth is  L_{\max} :

|\mathcal{A}| \leq p^{L_{\max}}

Proof. Each derivation is a sequence of at most  L_{\max}  rule applications with at most  p  choices each. Different sequences may yield the same hypergraph, so this is an upper bound.  \square 

Full-flow staged combustion verification. Full-flow staged combustion requires:

1. Two independent preburner derivations (fuel-rich and oxidizer-rich);
2. Two independent turbine derivations;
3. Both turbine shafts mechanically coupled to the corresponding pumps;
4. Both preburner exhausts routed to the main chamber.

This topology is a strict superset of gas-generator and single-preburner staged combustion topologies. The grammar must generate it with  L_{\max} \leq 12 . If not,  L_{\max}  is increased.

