Appendix B: Hypervolume Theory

B.1 Formal Definition

Definition B.1 (Hypervolume Indicator). For a finite approximation set  A \subset \mathbb{R}^m  with reference point  \mathbf{r} \in \mathbb{R}^m  dominating all elements of  A :

\mathcal{H}(A, \mathbf{r}) = \Lambda\left(\bigcup_{\mathbf{a} \in A} [\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{r}]\right)

where  \Lambda  is the Lebesgue measure and  [\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{r}] = \{\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^m : a_i \leq x_i \leq r_i, \forall i\} .

B.2 Pareto Compliance Proof

Theorem B.1 (Pareto Compliance). If  A \succ B  (A dominates B), then  \mathcal{H}(A, \mathbf{r}) \geq \mathcal{H}(B, \mathbf{r})  for any  \mathbf{r}  dominating all elements of  A \cup B .

Proof. If  A \succ B , then for every  \mathbf{b} \in B , there exists  \mathbf{a} \in A  such that  \mathbf{a} \leq \mathbf{b}  component-wise. The hypervolume contribution of each  \mathbf{b}  is  [\mathbf{b}, \mathbf{r}] \subseteq [\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{r}] . Hence:

\bigcup_{\mathbf{b} \in B} [\mathbf{b}, \mathbf{r}] \subseteq \bigcup_{\mathbf{a} \in A} [\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{r}]

Taking Lebesgue measure:  \mathcal{H}(B, \mathbf{r}) \leq \mathcal{H}(A, \mathbf{r}) .  \square 

B.3 Reference Point Selection

Normalization. All objective values are normalized to  [0,1]  using problem-specific physical bounds:

\tilde{f}_k = \frac{f_k - f_k^{\min}}{f_k^{\max} - f_k^{\min}}

where  f_k^{\min}  and  f_k^{\max}  are derived from engineering constraints (e.g., minimum acceptable thrust, maximum allowable mass), not from pooled run data.

Reference point. The reference point is fixed at  \mathbf{r} = (1.1, 1.1, \ldots, 1.1)  in normalized space. Sensitivity analysis is performed for  \mathbf{r} = (1.05, \ldots, 1.05)  and  \mathbf{r} = (1.20, \ldots, 1.20) .

Justification. The hypervolume indicator is a set measure used in evolutionary multiobjective optimization to evaluate search algorithm performance. The shape of the Pareto front and its slope determines how points maximizing the hypervolume indicator are distributed; the reference point must be chosen such that the extremes of the front can be found.

B.4 High-Dimensional Hypervolume Computation

The computation of hypervolume requires exponential time in the number of objectives. For thousands of points in more than 5 dimensions, exact computation is infeasible. Monte Carlo sampling provides a practical approximation strategy for high-dimensional settings.

Monte Carlo hypervolume estimation. The hypervolume is estimated by:

1. Generate  M  uniformly random points in the bounding box  [\mathbf{a}_{\min}, \mathbf{r}] ;
2. Count the fraction  \hat{p}  that are dominated by at least one element of  A ;
3. Estimate  \mathcal{H}(A, \mathbf{r}) \approx \hat{p} \cdot \text{Vol}([\mathbf{a}_{\min}, \mathbf{r}]) .

The standard error is  \sigma_{\mathcal{H}} = \text{Vol} \cdot \sqrt{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})/M} . For  M = 10^6 , the relative error is typically below 0.1% for 6-dimensional problems.

