Appendix C: Statistical Framework

C.1 Mann–Whitney U Test

Definition C.1. For two independent samples  X_1, \ldots, X_{K_1}  and  Y_1, \ldots, Y_{K_2} , the test statistic is:

U = \sum_{i=1}^{K_1} \sum_{j=1}^{K_2} \mathbb{1}[X_i > Y_j] + \frac{1}{2}\mathbb{1}[X_i = Y_j]

Null distribution. Under  H_0  (no difference), the expected value is  \mathbb{E}[U] = K_1 K_2 / 2  and the variance (no ties) is:

\text{Var}(U) = \frac{K_1 K_2 (K_1 + K_2 + 1)}{12}

For  K_1 = K_2 = K :

\sigma_U = \sqrt{\frac{K^2 (2K+1)}{12}}

C.2 Vargha–Delaney Effect Size

Definition C.2. The  A_{12}  effect size is:

A_{12} = \frac{U}{K_1 K_2}

This estimates the probability that a randomly selected observation from the LLM-seeded distribution exceeds a randomly selected observation from the random distribution.

C.3 Corrected Power Analysis

Theorem C.1 (Power of Mann–Whitney U test). For  n_1 = n_2 = K  and  A_{12} = p , the power at significance level  \alpha  is approximately:

\text{Power} \approx \Phi\left( \frac{K^2(p - 0.5)}{\sigma_U} - z_{1-\alpha/2} \right)

where  \Phi  is the standard normal CDF and  z_{1-\alpha/2}  is the two-sided critical value.

Application to V0.4. For  K = 30 ,  \sigma_U \approx 67.6 . Under  A_{12} = 0.65 , expected  U \approx 585 , difference from null 450 is 135. For two-sided  \alpha = 0.05 :

\text{Power} = P(Z > 1.96 - 135/67.6) = P(Z > -0.04) \approx 0.52

Thus power is approximately 52%, not 80%. To achieve 80% power for  A_{12} = 0.65 , we require  K \approx 55  per arm.

V0.4 decision. Adopt  K = 50  independent runs per arm to achieve ≥ 75% power for  A_{12} = 0.65  at  \alpha = 0.05 . For  A_{12} = 0.71  (large effect),  K = 30  suffices.

C.4 Multiple Comparison Correction

For multi-arm experiments (LLM one-shot, LLM iterative, random, heuristic, Bayesian optimization), apply Kruskal–Wallis followed by Dunn's post-hoc test with Bonferroni correction.

Kruskal–Wallis statistic:

H = \frac{12}{N(N+1)} \sum_{i=1}^{g} \frac{R_i^2}{n_i} - 3(N+1)

where  N = \sum n_i ,  R_i  is the sum of ranks in group  i , and  g  is the number of groups.

Dunn's post-hoc with Bonferroni. For each pair  (i,j) :

z_{ij} = \frac{\bar{R}_i - \bar{R}_j}{\sqrt{\frac{N(N+1)}{12}\left(\frac{1}{n_i} + \frac{1}{n_j}\right)}}

with adjusted p-value  p_{ij}^{\text{adj}} = \min(1, m \cdot p_{ij})  where  m  is the number of pairwise comparisons.

C.5 Secondary Cost Metrics

Because LLM calls consume API tokens and wall-clock time:

\mathcal{H}_{\text{cost}} = \frac{\mathcal{H}}{C_{\text{total}}}

where:

C_{\text{total}} = c_{\text{solver}} N_{\text{solver}} + c_{\text{token}} T_{\text{LLM}} + c_{\text{wall}} \tau_{\text{wall}}

Token usage, number of LLM calls, and wall-clock time are logged for every run.

