Appendix E: NASA PUMPA Meanline Pump Model

E.1 Model Basis

The PUMPA code is based on the Euler equation coupled with empirical correlations for rotor efficiency. The design-point rotor efficiency is obtained from empirically derived correlations of loss to rotor specific speed. The code has empirically derived rotor loss correlations to specific speed based on tests of several rocket engine turbopumps and research rigs.

E.2 Euler Equation

The Euler head rise is:

H = \frac{U_2 C_{\theta 2} - U_1 C_{\theta 1}}{g}

where  U  is blade speed,  C_\theta  is tangential absolute velocity, and subscripts 1 and 2 denote inlet and outlet.

E.3 Rotor Efficiency Correlation

The design-point rotor efficiency is:

\eta_p = f(N_s)

where  N_s  is the specific speed:

N_s = \frac{N \sqrt{Q}}{H^{3/4}}

with  N  in rpm,  Q  in m³/s, and  H  in m.

E.4 Off-Design Performance

The off-design variation of rotor efficiency is empirically derived from pump data for values of flow coefficient  F  between 0.7 and 1.2, with the extremities extrapolated. The code can generate pump maps for:

· Liquid hydrogen;
· Liquid oxygen;
· Liquid nitrogen;
· JP-4;
· Water;
· Air.

Fluid properties are obtained from GASPLUS.

E.5 Cavitation Model

The suction performance at off-design conditions is based on empirical correlations to the suction performance at design. The cavitation inception point along a speedline is where static pressure equals vapor pressure.

