Appendix F: NSGA-II with ε-Constraint Handling and Repair

F.1 NSGA-II Algorithm

NSGA-II (Non-dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm II) uses:

1. Fast non-dominated sorting;
2. Crowding distance for diversity preservation;
3. Elitist selection.

Fast non-dominated sorting. Each solution  p  has:

· Domination count  n_p  (number of solutions dominating  p );
· Set  S_p  of solutions dominated by  p .

Solutions with  n_p = 0  form the first Pareto front. Remove them, decrement counts, repeat.

Crowding distance. For each objective  m , sort solutions by  f_m , assign infinite distance to boundary solutions, and:

d_i = d_i + \frac{f_m(i+1) - f_m(i-1)}{f_m^{\max} - f_m^{\min}}

F.2 ε-Constraint Handling

ε-NSGA-II adds ε-dominance and adaptive population sizing to reduce parameter specification needs. ε-dominance does not allow two solutions with a difference less than ε in each objective to dominate one another. This enhances coverage and diversity along the full extent of tradeoffs.

Adaptive ε for equality constraints. Equality constraints are relaxed to:

|h_j(A, \mathbf{x})| \leq \epsilon_{\text{eq}}(t)

where  \epsilon_{\text{eq}}(t)  decreases from  10^{-1}  to  10^{-6}  over generations.

Parameter selection for ε-NSGA-II. The four major parameters are:

1. Probability of mating;
2. Probability of mutation;
3. Maximum run time;
4. Initial population size.

F.3 Repair Operator (Applied Symmetrically to All Arms)

A repair operator fixes solutions that violate constraints. The proposed repair operator is used to fix solutions that violate box constraints and has been integrated into NSGA-II and MOEA/D.

Gradient-based projection.

1. Compute constraint violation:
   \phi(\mathbf{z}) = \sum_j \max(0, g_j(\mathbf{z})) + \sum_k |h_k(\mathbf{z})|
2. If  \phi > 0 , compute gradient  \nabla \phi  (finite differences for graph components);
3. Project  \mathbf{z}  onto feasible set via constrained gradient descent (5 iterations).

Symmetry requirement. All experimental arms (LLM, random, heuristic, Bayesian optimization) receive identical repair operators. Any performance difference is attributable to proposal quality, not asymmetric access to repair.

F.4 Constraint-Domination

A solution  i  constraint-dominates solution  j  if:

1.  i  is feasible and  j  is infeasible; OR
2. Both are infeasible and  i  has smaller constraint violation; OR
3. Both are feasible and  i  dominates  j  in objective space.

