Appendix G: Structural and Rotordynamic Models

G.1 Structural Constraints

Hoop stress (thin-wall theory).

\sigma_\theta = \frac{P_c R}{t} \leq \frac{\sigma_{\text{yield}}}{FS}

where  FS = 1.5  is the factor of safety and  \sigma_{\text{yield}}  is temperature-dependent yield strength with  \sigma = 5\%  uncertainty.

Classical shell buckling.

P_{\text{cr}} = \frac{2E}{\sqrt{3(1-\nu^2)}} \left(\frac{t}{R}\right)^2

Constraint:  P_{\text{external}} \leq P_{\text{cr}} .

Fatigue life (simplified Coffin–Manson).

N_{\text{cycles}} = C \left(\frac{\Delta \epsilon_p}{2}\right)^{-\alpha}

Constraint:  N_{\text{cycles}} \geq N_{\text{mission}} .

Flange load.

F_{\text{flange}} \leq F_{\text{allow}}

G.2 Rotordynamic Constraints

Critical speed analysis. Numerical analyses of critical speed and mass unbalance response are performed for rocket turbopumps. The stiffness and damping of ball bearings and non-contact seals are quantified under aerodynamic and hydrodynamic loads. The rear bearing stiffness is the most important parameter for critical speed and instability because the first vibration mode is typically a turbine-side shaft bending mode.

Critical speed constraint.

|N_{\text{operating}} - N_{\text{critical},i}| \geq \Delta N_{\text{margin}}, \qquad \Delta N_{\text{margin}} = 0.20 N_{\text{operating}}

Bearing load constraint.

F_{\text{bearing}} \leq F_{\text{bearing,allow}}

Rotor stability. The logarithmic decrement  \delta  must satisfy  \delta > 0  at the operating speed, computed from complex eigenvalue analysis of the rotor–bearing–seal system.

Transfer matrix method. A one-dimensional transfer matrix with lumped masses, bearing stiffness/damping, and seal cross-coupling coefficients. Bearing housing structural flexibility significantly reduces rotor critical speed and onset speed of instability.

