Appendix H: Entropy Generation: Validity Gate vs. Optimization Objective

H.1 Validity Gate

For every control volume  C , the component model must satisfy:

\dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C} \geq -\varepsilon_{\text{num}}

where  \varepsilon_{\text{num}} = 10^{-6}  (relative to total entropy flow). If  \dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C} < -\varepsilon_{\text{num}} , the component model has produced a physically invalid result. The design is rejected from the optimization population and logged as a solver failure. This is a self-consistency check on the numerical model, not a thermodynamic quality metric.

H.2 Optimization Objective

For designs that pass the validity gate:

\dot{S}_{\text{gen}}^{\text{opt}} = \sum_{C: \text{valid}} \dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C}

This is minimized as an objective. Because the validity gate ensures  \dot{S}_{\text{gen}, C} \geq 0  for all included components, the optimizer cannot "learn to avoid" regions where the solver breaks — those regions are excluded before the objective is computed.

H.3 Component Entropy Generation

\dot{S}_{\text{gen},k} = \dot{m}_k (s_{\text{out}} - s_{\text{in}}) - \frac{\dot{Q}_k}{T_{\text{wall},k}}

Entropy-based performance analysis of chemical rockets shows that overall entropy generation is minimized at conditions corresponding to maximum endurance. The gross entropy generation of irreversibility processes is used as an optimization objective.

