Appendix M: Proofs and Theorems

M.1 Newton–Raphson Convergence for Shaft Matching

Theorem M.1. Let  \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{z}) = \mathbf{0}  be the shaft matching system, where  \mathbf{z} = (\dot{m}, \Pi, N) . If  \mathbf{F}  is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of the solution  \mathbf{z}^*  and the Jacobian  J(\mathbf{z}^*)  is nonsingular, then Newton–Raphson converges quadratically to  \mathbf{z}^* .

Proof. Standard Newton–Raphson convergence theorem for systems of nonlinear equations. The Jacobian nonsingularity condition is checked numerically; if  \text{cond}(J) > 10^6 , the shaft is rejected as ill-conditioned (physically corresponding to operation near the cavitation limit).  \square 

M.2 Entropy Generation Non-Negativity

Theorem M.2. For any control volume satisfying mass, momentum, and energy conservation,  \dot{S}_{\text{gen}} \geq 0 .

Proof. Direct statement of the second law of thermodynamics. Any component model predicting  \dot{S}_{\text{gen}} < 0  violates the Clausius inequality and is rejected.  \square 

M.3 Grammar-Generated Design Space Finiteness

Theorem M.3. If the grammar  \mathcal{G}  has no recursive production rules, then  |\mathcal{A}| < \infty .

Proof. In the absence of recursion, every derivation has length bounded by the maximum depth of the grammar's production tree. Since each derivation step applies one of  p  rules,  |\mathcal{A}| \leq p^{L_{\max}} < \infty .  \square 

Note on recursion. Rule  p_5  (FeedLine → Valve → FeedLine) is recursive. Finiteness is ensured by the explicit bound  L_{\max} , which truncates derivations. This is a design-space truncation imposed by the researcher, not a theorem.

M.4 Port-Type Safety (Restated)

Theorem M.4. If all productions of  \mathcal{G}  are port-consistent, then every derived architecture  A \in \mathcal{A}  has port-type-consistent connections.

Proof. By induction on derivation length (see Appendix A.2).  \square 

M.5 Isomorphism Collapse Bound (Restated)

Proposition M.1.  |\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}| \leq p^{L_{\max}} , with equality iff no two distinct derivations yield isomorphic hypergraphs.

Proof. The map from derivation sequences to isomorphism classes is surjective.  \square 

